+        <div class="journal-text" id="journal-text">
+          <p>Every bag of coffee that leaves our roastery starts with a relationship, not a transaction. Long before
+            a single bean meets our drum roaster, we have spent months, and in many cases years, getting to know the
+            people who grow it. We work with a small number of farms and cooperatives in Ethiopia, Colombia,
+            Guatemala, and Sumatra, and we visit each of them at least once a year during harvest. Those trips are
+            where the real work happens: walking the rows with producers, tasting cherries straight off the branch,
+            and talking honestly about what went well last season and what didn't.</p>
+
+          <h3>Direct Trade, Fair Prices</h3>
+          <p>We buy the majority of our green coffee through direct trade, which means we negotiate with farmers
+            and cooperatives ourselves instead of purchasing anonymous lots through a commodity broker. That lets us
+            pay well above Fairtrade minimums, typically between two and three times the commodity market price,
+            and it lets us commit to multi-year purchasing agreements. Those commitments matter. A farmer who knows
+            we'll be back next year can invest in better drying beds, new varietals, or fair wages for pickers
+            without gambling on whether a buyer will show up.</p>
+
+          <h3>Quality Starts at the Cupping Table</h3>
+          <p>Before we commit to any lot, we cup it, sometimes dozens of times. Producers send us small pre-shipment
+            samples, which our team roasts lightly and evaluates blind for sweetness, acidity, body, and clarity. We
+            only buy coffees scoring 85 points or higher on the Specialty Coffee Association scale, and we look
+            beyond the score for character: a Yirgacheffe that tastes like bergamot and stone fruit, or a Huehuetenango
+            with a deep, cocoa-driven finish. When a sample doesn't meet the mark, we share our notes with the
+            producer so we can work on it together for next year.</p>
+
+          <h3>Sustainability on the Farm</h3>
+          <p>Our partners grow coffee the way it has been grown for generations: under shade canopies that protect
+            the soil, support bird habitats, and slow the ripening of the cherry for more complex flavor. Many of our
+            farms are certified organic, and those that aren't still follow low-input practices we review together on
+            each visit. We also fund a small community grant each year, chosen by the cooperatives themselves. Past
+            grants have paid for a water filtration system in Sidama and a new school roof in Nariño.</p>
+
+          <h3>From Port to Roaster</h3>
+          <p>Green coffee is shipped in GrainPro-lined jute bags that protect it from moisture and odors during the
+            long ocean crossing. When it arrives at our roastery, we test every lot for moisture content and density
+            before it goes into climate-controlled storage. We roast in small batches of no more than thirty pounds,
+            developing a unique profile for each origin, and we ship within forty-eight hours of roasting so the coffee
+            reaches you at its peak.</p>
+
+          <p>None of this is the fastest or cheapest way to make coffee. But we believe a great cup should be good for
+            everyone involved, from the family that picked the cherries to the person drinking it on a slow Saturday
+            morning. Thanks for being part of that chain.</p>
